Behcet's Disease (Mild Mucocutaneous)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Painful oral ulcers without genital or ocular involvement, manageable topically

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-005
Reason for Visit: Acute eruption of severe oral ulcers preventing fluid intake and causing dehydration risk.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute oral mucosal failure preventing hydration. Prompt assessment was necessary to administer robust analgesia and assess hydration status, preventing acute dehydration and potential need for intravenous fluids prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My mouth is full of ulcers and I haven't been able to drink a drop of water all day because of the pain.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute flare of recurrent isolated oral aphthous ulcers beginning 48 hours ago. The ulcers have multiplied rapidly today, causing severe odynophagia. Patient has been unable to tolerate oral fluids for the past 12 hours and complains of mild dizziness when standing.
Functional Status: Independent, but fatigued due to lack of intake.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, risk of dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, dry lips, speaking with difficulty due to oral pain.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal exam reveals multiple 3-5mm erythematous aphthous ulcers on the buccal mucosa and soft palate. Painful oral ulcers without genital or ocular involvement, manageable topically. Mucous membranes are slightly tacky. Capillary refill is 2 seconds. Skin turgor is adequate. No cervical lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Appropriate access to fluids in the kitchen, safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Using over-the-counter paracetamol with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Behcet's Disease (Mild Mucocutaneous) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Herpes Simplex Virus Stomatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of vesicular lesions, no gingival swelling, and negative history of cold sores.

Hand, Foot, and Mouth Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of corresponding lesions on the palms or soles and no significant fever.

Severe Dehydration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Maintained blood pressure, adequate skin turgor, and clear mentation despite reduced intake.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to living alone with reduced oral intake)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe odynophagia secondary to acute mucocutaneous ulceration, threatening oral hydration. Triage correctly identified the risk of impending dehydration requiring immediate intervention. Clinical assessment confirmed mild volume depletion without hemodynamic compromise. Immediate administration of topical anesthetic and corticosteroid paste was imperative to restore the patient's ability to swallow fluids. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have likely resulted in significant dehydration requiring hospital admission and intravenous fluid resuscitation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical lignocaine viscous and topical corticosteroid paste (e.g., triamcinolone acetonide). Trial of oral fluids post-application was successful.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply the numbing paste precisely 10 minutes before meals and drink one full glass of cold water immediately after. By linking the pain relief directly to fluid intake, you will successfully rehydrate yourself and avoid a hospital admission.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to monitor ulcer resolution.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you become completely unable to swallow saliva, experience severe dizziness on standing that results in a fall, or develop a high fever, you must present to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: With potent topical therapy, oral pain should reduce significantly within 24 hours, allowing normal hydration. Ulcers typically heal in 7-10 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if you have not passed urine for more than 12 hours, feel progressively lightheaded, or develop new lesions in your eyes or genital region.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The topical treatments will numb the nerve endings and directly suppress the immune flare in the mouth, allowing you to drink safely.
Actionable Steps: Consistently apply the paste before drinking. Stick to cool, non-acidic fluids like water or milk to avoid further irritation.

